  {
    "episode_id": 84720,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, not really. I don't think that, you know, I don't think that humanism is a kind of false line. It's certainly not a political party or a faction. And of course, most of our political divisions are organized around partisanship. You know, our founders were people who wanted to break from centuries of religious conflict and religious warfare in public spaces. They were rebelling against the history of holy crusades and inquisitions and theocratic government and witchcraft trials and all of that. Our Constitution, of course, doesn't mention God. Jefferson and Madison and Tom Payne and Ben Franklin and our major founders were people who thought that union of government and religion had been a disaster for mankind. And they wanted to separate church and state, create a wall of separation in Jefferson's phrase. And so I think that it behooves everybody to be a humanist in public things to try to measure our progress by virtue of how well we're increasing public welfare and utility. And privately people are, of course, entitled to their own religious, spiritual, theological beliefs and ideas. But in America, we don't impose religious tests on public office. That's in the Constitution. And we also don't impose religious programs through government. That's what we're struggling against in many parts of the world.",
