    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that's right. The establishment clause, which says the government shall not establish religion, is closely tied to the free exercise clause, because if you allow one religion, whether it's Puritanism, or Seventh-day Adventism, or Islam, or Branch Davidianism, whatever it might be, to seize the power of the state and to impose a religious program, that ends up squelching the religious freedom. rights of worship that belong to each individual. And so one important argument for the separation of church and state is religious free exercise. But the other, of course, is that our ability to make progress in public things through government in the public space depends on the sovereignty of a reason. And we need to be able to speak in public in a language that cuts across the lines of division of religion, as well as Of course, ethnicity and race and gender and all of the other artificial barriers that have been thrown up to human progress and social unity.",
