    "turn_text": "Well, of course, there's nothing wrong with it from the standpoint of any particular individual. I mean, someone might want to think we're one nation under God, someone might want to think we're one nation under Allah, one person might want to think that we're one nation under Canada. You know, different people can have different points of view on that. The question is, do we allow the government to compel people or compel school children specifically to say that we are one nation under God, or Jesus Christ, or Vishnu, or Allah, and of course the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals, and the New Deal case got it right and said that the Pledge of Allegiance, as it was originally written by Francis Bellamy, was perfectly fine, and I know mention of God. It said, you know, I pledge allegiance to my flag of the United States of America and to the Republic for which it stands One Nation indivisible with liberty and justice for all. And Bellamy had written that as a very tough statement in 1892 against the continuing display of the Confederate battle flag. And so he wrote it as an angry kind of radical statement saying that we are One Nation, we're not divided between North and South, and we are dedicated to liberty and justice for all. And those were fighting words. when the Redeemer governments in the south were busily trying to restore white supremacy. And so the words, One Nation Under God, were added in 1954, interestingly just a few months after the Supreme Court's decision in Brown v. Board of Education. And President Eisenhower in his signing statement said that this would be a message to the communist world, that God was on the side of the free world and the free world was on the side of God. And so it was an explicitly religious notion. And when the Supreme Court had struck down a compulsory pledge of allegiance back in West Virginia versus Barnett. And this was even without one nation under God. The, you know, the court had said basically we don't compel people to profess particular beliefs. And when the 9th Circuit Court of Appeals and the New Delta case struck it down with one nation under God, you know, it basically said that this is very clearly a religious imposition. And the government cannot endorse monotheism any more than it can endorse polytheism or Christianity over Islam or Hinduism or any other religion. So, you know, my proposal is if we need to be one nation under anything, we should be one nation under the Constitution because we're not unified by one particular religious orthodoxy or political philosophy or race or ethnicity, but we are unified by having one Constitution And it's under that Constitution, including the First Amendment, that everybody should be able to rally and unify.",
