    "turn_text": "and it will undoubtedly come back again. And I think that if the Supreme Court follows the logic of its own rulings, it will have to go with what the Ninth Circuit said in the case below, in the Court of Appeals. And incidentally, that case was written by a conservative Republican appointee to the bench. I mean, it's just obvious that no matter which establishment clause test you use, Lemon versus Kurtzman is the standard one, it fails. Lemon versus Kurtzman says that in order for enactment to pass establishment clause scrutiny, you need to have a secular purpose, a secular effect, and no entanglement of church and state, but one nation under God in the Pledge of Allegiance has an entirely religious purpose, an entirely religious effect in terms of promoting the religious concept, and it does entangle church and state causing lots of sectarian conflict in the schools and we know that there's lots of conflict over one nation under God. So there are other tests too, the endorsement test which is Justice O'Connor as proposed but all of them lead back to the same conclusion which is that this is just a religious idea and if you're an originalist why not go back to the original Pledge of Allegiance the way that Francis Bellamy wrote it. on the 400th anniversary of Columbus's arrival in the new world. This was sort of his tribute to Columbus,",
