  {
    "episode_id": 84720,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think so. Look, I think that the basic Supreme Court doctrine is moving in the right direction. There was a disappointing case about religious invocation the town of Greece case this year where the majority found that an overwhelmingly sectarian practice of religious invocation was acceptable and there were real factual problems with the way that it was taking place. But I think in general, if you compare America to what's happening around the world, we continue to stand very tall in terms of the separation of church and state. Now, we know there's a constant undertow and there's a, you know, a constant attack on it. One of my colleagues in the Maryland legislature wanted to follow Texas and Alabama and some other states endorsing the Ten Commandments. And, you know, I basically said, you know, the Ten Commandments have been fine for centuries without a formal endorsement by the Maryland General Assembly, but I promise that if they insisted on bringing it back that I would try to move to amend it to require that we vote on each commandment separately. And that people not be allowed to vote for the whole Ten Commandment package on the grounds that people would be voting on commandments that they themselves have violated and then we'd be taking the name of the Lord in vain. I think that it's always tempting for politicians rather than dealing with real problems in the crises that face us like climate change and continuing environmental degradation to score points with voters just by endorsing various religious ideas or texts. And I think that that's a form of cheap political symbolism and the voters ultimately can see through that.",
