    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 74,
    "turn_text": "Well, look, I mean, this is the, it's not even an issue, really. This is the mega reality that confronts us in everything that we do. And if our brains were not limited by two small frontal cortex and if we were able to have the big picture in mind and we could all think in a fifty or a hundred-year horizon we would all be working on this all the time because the scientists tell us that we are facing imminent peril in terms of the future habitability of the earth because of the the increases in the temperature and the rise in oceans and the various weather calamities associated with climate change. So I think that anybody who's a humanist, that is anybody who believes in the possibilities and the promise of humanity and the need for the species to survive has got to be working on this issue. And it does. Now, we should say that there are lots of religious people who are coming around to the necessity of acting on this, lots of them across the political spectrum. But of course, there are also those who have a theological position that human beings could not be affecting the climate, could not be affecting the oceans in the temperature it's all in the hands of god there's nothing that we can do and so we should just um... forget about it or stick our heads in the sand or join the climate change deniers uh... and certainly there are those who believe that uh... the earth is going to end before fifty or a hundred years",
