  {
    "episode_id": 84720,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 97,
    "turn_text": "Well, in Roe versus Wade in 1973, Justice Blackman writing for the majority developed this trimester framework where basically a woman had a right to choose an abortion and consultation with her physician during the first trimester. And then in the second trimester, the right could be regulated in the interests of the health of the mother. In the third trimester, the states could outright ban abortion. That trimester framework was replaced in the Planned Parenthood versus Casey decision based really on science and developments in technology and their Justice O'Connor wrote a decision for the majority moving up the point of potential regulation to the the point of fetal viability outside of the mother's body. And so this is allowed for closer regulation of the abortion decision and the banning of abortion after the point of fetal viability while still retaining the core of the Roe versus Wade Wright, which is that in the earlier months, the woman retains a presumptive right to choose an abortion. So science has altered um... the jurisprudence there and i think that we have achieved a kind of social and political equilibrium on this issue i do not think that the american people uh... will accept the idea that the government will ban and criminalize abortion and make doctors or women who engage in abortions in the criminals i just don't think that can happen at the same time um... the development of the law has been such that There have been tighter limits imposed on the abortion decision.",
