  {
    "episode_id": 84720,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 101,
    "turn_text": "And so I think we have an obligation to do what we can to advance the public health by dramatically reducing the casualties at the same time that we respect the Second Amendment rights that have been identified by the Supreme Court in the Heller decision. In some sense, the Supreme Court in Heller, which was a 5-4 case, did us a favor because it said, OK, there is an individual right to bear arms, but that right cannot be absolute, like other rights, for example, the abortion right. It is subject to reasonable regulations in the interest of the public safety. And so the law that we just passed in Maryland, which is a very tough law, relative to other states has been upheld so far with a ban on sale of new assault weapons, a fingerprint requirement for gun licensure, this 24-hour provision expanding the authority of the state police to inspect gun dealers, most of whom are law abiding, but some of whom have been dealing guns directly into the criminal underground. The NRA forces are categorically opposed to any regulation and have whipped up hysteria in different parts of the country about attempts to create a better balance in our gun laws. And I think that's a scandal. You know, we passed our law after the catastrophe in Newtown, Connecticut, where, you know, lunatic gun men went into a school and killed dozens of kids and other people and perhaps the most disturbing conversation I've had in my time in public office was with someone who told me that he was totally opposed to any new regulation of guns and that he doubted whether the Newtown massacre even took place",
