  {
    "episode_id": 84720,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84720.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 108,
    "turn_text": "and so on. Right. Yeah. One of the ideas in critical thinking is if you're looking at an issue, go to extremes and see the problems in the issue. And I think that's a good point for gun control. When we say that we're allowed to bear arms or the Constitution protects our right to bear arms, We have to think about when that was written and in what context and what arms was actually available at that time and think about the extreme like what if we all had a button like a button that launched a nuclear missile and that's technically an arm as well. Well, obviously that's one extreme and we could see that something like that for every person to be able to own and to have a trigger on them at all times that could destroy the world is just would just be crazy. So we know that there's a balance somewhere in between and it's not an absolute position.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "One idea in critical thinking is to examine issues by considering their extremes.",
